1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,052
A fire dies out with wood still left.

2
00:00:03,052 --> 00:00:05,543
A sandwich stall runs out of cheese.

3
00:00:05,543 --> 00:00:08,033
A bicycle shop runs short of wheels.

4
00:00:08,433 --> 00:00:12,670
In each case, one thing runs out
first, and it decides how much you can

5
00:00:12,670 --> 00:00:13,570
make.

6
00:00:13,625 --> 00:00:16,381
Aarav sees this in his college cycle club.

7
00:00:16,381 --> 00:00:18,883
They have seven frames and ten wheels.

8
00:00:19,233 --> 00:00:22,042
Each bicycle needs a frame and two wheels.

9
00:00:22,042 --> 00:00:25,316
So only five get made,
and two frames are left.

10
00:00:25,816 --> 00:00:27,453
The wheels set the limit.

11
00:00:27,453 --> 00:00:29,225
Reactions work the same way.

12
00:00:29,875 --> 00:00:31,967
Chemistry has the same idea.

13
00:00:32,317 --> 00:00:36,488
The limiting reagent is the
reactant that gets used up first.

14
00:00:36,488 --> 00:00:39,406
Reagent is just another word for reactant.

15
00:00:39,806 --> 00:00:42,187
Once it is gone, the reaction stops.

16
00:00:42,187 --> 00:00:44,910
So it limits how much
product you can make.

17
00:00:45,310 --> 00:00:47,454
The other reactant is left over.

18
00:00:47,454 --> 00:00:49,502
We call it the excess reagent.

19
00:00:50,152 --> 00:00:53,077
Let's see it on our molecule
bench, with ammonia.

20
00:00:53,427 --> 00:00:58,575
Two nitrogen molecules, each locked
by three shared pairs of electrons.

21
00:00:58,575 --> 00:01:00,765
And three hydrogen molecules.

22
00:01:01,165 --> 00:01:05,270
One nitrogen molecule needs
three hydrogen molecules.

23
00:01:05,670 --> 00:01:06,570
Watch.

24
00:01:06,260 --> 00:01:09,784
The bonds break, the atoms
move, and new bonds form.

25
00:01:10,184 --> 00:01:11,887
Two ammonia molecules.

26
00:01:11,887 --> 00:01:14,525
But now every hydrogen is used up.

27
00:01:14,925 --> 00:01:17,867
The second nitrogen has no partner left.

28
00:01:17,867 --> 00:01:20,291
Hydrogen is the limiting reagent.

29
00:01:20,291 --> 00:01:22,043
Nitrogen is in excess.

30
00:01:22,693 --> 00:01:26,280
To use both nitrogens, we
would need six hydrogens.

31
00:01:26,280 --> 00:01:27,782
We have only three.

32
00:01:28,132 --> 00:01:30,373
Here is the method for any problem.

33
00:01:30,723 --> 00:01:31,623
Step one.

34
00:01:31,557 --> 00:01:33,481
Write the balanced equation.

35
00:01:34,231 --> 00:01:35,143
Step two.

36
00:01:35,143 --> 00:01:37,825
Turn each reactant's mass into moles.

37
00:01:38,575 --> 00:01:39,518
Step three.

38
00:01:39,518 --> 00:01:41,997
Divide each by its number in front.

39
00:01:41,997 --> 00:01:43,780
The smallest is limiting.

40
00:01:44,530 --> 00:01:45,430
Step four.

41
00:01:45,430 --> 00:01:49,102
Use only the limiting reagent
to work out the product.

42
00:01:49,852 --> 00:01:51,435
Now compare two cases.

43
00:01:51,435 --> 00:01:54,193
In each, twenty eight grams of nitrogen.

44
00:01:54,193 --> 00:01:55,528
That is one mole.

45
00:01:55,928 --> 00:01:59,310
Case A, four grams of hydrogen, two moles.

46
00:01:59,310 --> 00:02:00,962
Nitrogen needs three.

47
00:02:00,962 --> 00:02:02,848
Hydrogen runs out first.

48
00:02:03,248 --> 00:02:06,332
Case B, ten grams of hydrogen, five moles.

49
00:02:06,332 --> 00:02:08,113
Two moles are left over.

50
00:02:08,513 --> 00:02:09,784
Pause and think.

51
00:02:09,784 --> 00:02:12,760
In case B, hydrogen has the smaller mass.

52
00:02:12,760 --> 00:02:15,406
Is hydrogen still the limiting reagent?

53
00:02:18,606 --> 00:02:19,506
No!

54
00:02:19,116 --> 00:02:20,191
Nitrogen is.

55
00:02:20,191 --> 00:02:24,073
Five moles of hydrogen is
more than the three needed.

56
00:02:24,073 --> 00:02:26,224
Compare moles, never grams.

57
00:02:26,874 --> 00:02:27,989
Remember this.

58
00:02:27,989 --> 00:02:32,272
The reactant that runs out first
decides how much product forms.

59
00:02:32,972 --> 00:02:35,853
Now, the worked example
from your NCERT book.

60
00:02:36,153 --> 00:02:40,673
Fifty kilograms of nitrogen and ten
kilograms of hydrogen are mixed to

61
00:02:40,673 --> 00:02:41,753
make ammonia.

62
00:02:41,753 --> 00:02:43,923
Which is the limiting reagent?

63
00:02:44,423 --> 00:02:45,323
Nitrogen.

64
00:02:45,272 --> 00:02:50,152
Fifty thousand grams divided by twenty
eight is about one thousand seven

65
00:02:50,152 --> 00:02:52,095
hundred eighty six moles.

66
00:02:52,845 --> 00:02:53,745
Hydrogen.

67
00:02:53,713 --> 00:02:58,828
NCERT uses two point zero one six
here, giving about four thousand nine

68
00:02:58,828 --> 00:03:00,461
hundred sixty moles.

69
00:03:01,211 --> 00:03:05,102
All that nitrogen would need
three times as much hydrogen.

70
00:03:05,102 --> 00:03:08,557
About five thousand three
hundred fifty seven moles.

71
00:03:08,557 --> 00:03:09,677
We have less.

72
00:03:10,427 --> 00:03:12,880
So hydrogen is the limiting reagent.

73
00:03:12,880 --> 00:03:15,546
Two thirds of its moles become ammonia.

74
00:03:15,546 --> 00:03:19,107
About three point three
times ten to the three moles.

75
00:03:19,857 --> 00:03:24,793
At seventeen grams per mole, that is
about fifty six kilograms of ammonia.

76
00:03:25,443 --> 00:03:29,181
Now one practice numerical
of our own, not from NCERT.

77
00:03:29,531 --> 00:03:33,488
Eight grams of methane burns
in sixteen grams of oxygen.

78
00:03:33,488 --> 00:03:36,338
That is zero point five moles of each.

79
00:03:37,088 --> 00:03:39,558
Divide by the numbers in front.

80
00:03:39,558 --> 00:03:41,663
Methane, zero point five.

81
00:03:41,663 --> 00:03:44,362
Oxygen, only zero point two five.

82
00:03:44,362 --> 00:03:45,940
Oxygen is limiting.

83
00:03:46,690 --> 00:03:51,958
So only zero point two five moles of
methane burns, giving eleven grams

84
00:03:51,958 --> 00:03:53,522
of carbon dioxide.

85
00:03:54,272 --> 00:03:56,315
Four grams of methane are left.

86
00:03:56,315 --> 00:03:59,416
Check: eleven, plus nine
of water, plus four.

87
00:03:59,416 --> 00:04:01,364
Twenty four grams in and out.

88
00:04:02,014 --> 00:04:03,480
Now, the exam trap.

89
00:04:03,830 --> 00:04:07,478
Some students call the reactant
with fewer moles limiting.

90
00:04:07,478 --> 00:04:10,118
One mole of nitrogen, two of hydrogen?

91
00:04:10,118 --> 00:04:11,581
Nitrogen, they say.

92
00:04:11,981 --> 00:04:12,881
Wrong.

93
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:14,973
Divide by the numbers in front.

94
00:04:14,973 --> 00:04:16,237
Nitrogen, one.

95
00:04:16,237 --> 00:04:18,722
Hydrogen, two over three, less.

96
00:04:18,722 --> 00:04:20,321
Hydrogen is limiting.

97
00:04:20,971 --> 00:04:22,401
Where is this asked?

98
00:04:22,401 --> 00:04:26,729
As numericals with two masses: find
the limiting reagent, the product,

99
00:04:26,729 --> 00:04:27,958
and the leftover.

100
00:04:28,558 --> 00:04:29,910
Exam level now.

101
00:04:29,910 --> 00:04:35,456
Ten grams of hydrogen reacts with sixty
four grams of oxygen to form water.

102
00:04:35,456 --> 00:04:37,519
What mass of water forms?

103
00:04:37,519 --> 00:04:38,928
Pause and solve.

104
00:04:44,328 --> 00:04:45,749
The answer is B.

105
00:04:45,749 --> 00:04:47,214
Seventy two grams.

106
00:04:47,714 --> 00:04:50,913
Two H two plus O two gives two H two O.

107
00:04:50,913 --> 00:04:52,747
Hydrogen is five moles.

108
00:04:52,747 --> 00:04:54,413
Oxygen is two moles.

109
00:04:54,913 --> 00:04:57,366
Divide by the numbers in front.

110
00:04:57,366 --> 00:04:59,456
Hydrogen, two point five.

111
00:04:59,456 --> 00:05:00,671
Oxygen, two.

112
00:05:00,671 --> 00:05:02,238
Oxygen is limiting.

113
00:05:02,738 --> 00:05:05,710
So four moles of water, seventy two grams.

114
00:05:05,710 --> 00:05:09,839
C just added the masses, but
two grams of hydrogen are left.

115
00:05:10,439 --> 00:05:11,900
Here is your recap.

116
00:05:11,900 --> 00:05:13,401
Screenshot this card.

117
00:05:16,601 --> 00:05:19,438
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

118
00:05:19,838 --> 00:05:20,738
One.

119
00:05:20,305 --> 00:05:22,900
What is a limiting reagent, in one line?

120
00:05:23,700 --> 00:05:24,600
Two.

121
00:05:24,197 --> 00:05:27,651
Why must you compare moles,
not grams, to find it?

122
00:05:28,451 --> 00:05:29,351
Three.

123
00:05:29,081 --> 00:05:33,110
Twenty eight grams of nitrogen
meets eight grams of hydrogen.

124
00:05:33,110 --> 00:05:34,866
Which one is left over?

125
00:05:35,766 --> 00:05:38,319
Next time: what is inside an atom?

126
00:05:38,319 --> 00:05:42,403
We find out in discovery of
electrons, protons and neutrons.
